sub:assertion {
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/thompson1917-ch04-04> delphi:hasAssessment <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/assessment/thompson1917-ch04-04--grok> .
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/assessment/thompson1917-ch04-04--grok> a delphi:LLMClaimAssessment ;
    rdfs:label "Grok 4.7 on thompson1917-ch04-04: not-empirically-resolvable, resolvability 0.22" ;
    dct:subject <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/concept/dataset-resolvability-0.2.0> ;
    delphi:assessmentType "dataset-resolvability/0.2.0" ;
    delphi:hasScore "0.223"^^xsd:double ;
    delphi:hasVerdict "not-empirically-resolvable" ;
    delphi:hasConfidence "0.74"^^xsd:double ;
    dct:description "Morphological axes are easy to score, but the claim does not add a measurement that would be true of a force field and false of other polarity mechanisms. Particle chains are both the phenomenon and the supposed proof. The statement therefore has no discriminating empirical consequence." ;
    rdfs:comment "Proposed test with existing data: The claim identifies visible cell polarity with a polarised force distribution whose lines are the aligned particles. Molecular polarity, cytoskeletal tracks and a physical force field all satisfy that description, so no outcome separates the claim from its rivals." ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/agent/delphisci-resolvability-assessor-grok-4.7-high-fast> ;
    delphi:evidenceAgainst "Aligned organelles are the observation the wording treats as the evidence of the forces.", "Any successful polarity mechanism can be redescribed as dynamical polarity." .
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/assessment/thompson1917-ch04-04--grok--operationalizability> a delphi:LLMClaimAssessment ;
    rdfs:label "Operationalizability of thompson1917-ch04-04 by Grok 4.7: 0.2" ;
